WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.200
Netrin is a secreted guidance cue that helps growing axons build connections.

2
00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:13.960
In the developing spinal cord, commissural axons grow toward the midline and cross

3
00:00:13.960 --> 00:00:14.960
to the other side.

4
00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:17.120
Netrin helps guide that journey.

5
00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:23.060
In mice lacking netrin, spinal commissural axons take wrong routes, and several

6
00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:24.520
forebrain crossings are missing.

7
00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:28.680
How does this cue guide the moving tip of an axon, the growth cone?

8
00:00:29.733 --> 00:00:33.993
The first clue came from pieces of embryonic rat spinal cord in a gel.

9
00:00:34.813 --> 00:00:38.793
A piece of dorsal spinal cord alone sent out few or no axons.

10
00:00:39.553 --> 00:00:43.233
Put a piece of floor plate nearby, and axon bundles grew toward it.

11
00:00:43.933 --> 00:00:45.133
The pieces never touched.

12
00:00:45.953 --> 00:00:47.033
Something crossed the gel.

13
00:00:47.953 --> 00:00:52.473
Later, cells made to secrete netrin attracted commissural axons at a distance.

14
00:00:53.533 --> 00:00:57.413
But in the mouse embryo, much of the netrin that matters acts locally.

15
00:00:58.593 --> 00:01:02.813
Cells called ventricular zone progenitors make netrin and lay it down on the pial

16
00:01:02.813 --> 00:01:05.053
surface, along the axons' path.

17
00:01:05.873 --> 00:01:09.393
The growth cone can encounter the cue close to where it is laid down.

18
00:01:10.367 --> 00:01:15.307
Deleting netrin only from the floor plate left most spinal commissural axons on

19
00:01:15.307 --> 00:01:19.347
course. Deleting it from those progenitor cells caused errors.

20
00:01:20.327 --> 00:01:24.387
Later studies found that floor plate netrin still contributes, working with

21
00:01:24.387 --> 00:01:25.387
progenitor netrin.

22
00:01:26.167 --> 00:01:28.047
So the dish shows attraction at a distance.

23
00:01:28.947 --> 00:01:33.287
The mouse shows that local netrin matters too, with more than one source helping

24
00:01:33.287 --> 00:01:34.287
guide the route.

25
00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:37.220
Now ask how the growth cone reads netrin.

26
00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:40.580
Receptors on its surface help decide the response.

27
00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:43.160
One receptor is called DCC.

28
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:45.620
It usually mediates attraction.

29
00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:52.720
Netrin binds cells displaying DCC, and an antibody to DCC blocks netrin evoked

30
00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:54.820
outgrowth from spinal cord explants.

31
00:01:56.463 --> 00:01:58.343
UNC5 receptors add repulsion.

32
00:01:59.263 --> 00:02:04.323
In worms, forcing this receptor into touch neurons sent their axons away from the

33
00:02:04.323 --> 00:02:06.383
cue. The cue stayed the same.

34
00:02:07.303 --> 00:02:09.103
Changing the receptor changed the answer.

35
00:02:10.083 --> 00:02:15.623
Treat this as a starting point: DCC can also take part in repulsion, and netrin has

36
00:02:15.623 --> 00:02:17.143
other receptors and partners.

37
00:02:18.217 --> 00:02:22.617
One smaller point comes from frog spinal growth cones grown in a dish.

38
00:02:23.577 --> 00:02:28.317
Blocking cyclic AMP signalling changed attraction to netrin into repulsion.

39
00:02:29.177 --> 00:02:31.677
An antibody to DCC stopped both turns.

40
00:02:32.577 --> 00:02:35.557
This is a worked example, not a rule for every axon.
